=== Text #12 ===
> [The] overpayment scam is another time-[honored] classic that's made a successful leap to the[ online] world. It goes like this: A business (usually a small, seemingly vulnerable one) receives a check from a[ buyer], usually of a big-[ticket] item. But suddenly there's a hitch — the buyer has mistakenly written the check for more than the[ correct] amount. To avoid the trouble of writing another check, the buyer asks that the check be[ deposited] and the difference[ wired back] to[ them]. We all know[ where] this is going,[ right]? The check bounces, the wire transfer is[ gone], the scammer is[ unt]raceable and the business owner is also responsible for bank fees.
Over[payment] scams are pretty easy to avoid with just a few safeguards. There are so many warning signs with these cons that you should be[ able] to[ stop] things[ before] they[ start]. A no-check policy would instantly[ solve] the problem, and it's also[ best] to steer[ clear] of[ wire transfers]. If you must accept checks for some reason, always ask for all of the buyer's contact information and then double-check — or,[ better] yet, deal with them in[ person] and[ ask] for a check from a local bank. Wait[ for checks] to[ clear before you] use any of the funds and,[ above] all, never[ accept] a check[ for] more than the agreed-upon amount.

